"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs=globalThis.webpackChunkp_vs_np_docs||[]).push([[1515],{74061(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"diagonalization","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags/diagonalization","allTagsPath":"/azeon/p-vs-np/tags","count":4,"items":[{"id":"failed-proofs/diagonalization-techniques","title":"Diagonalization Techniques","description":"Cantor used it to show some infinities are bigger than others. Turing used it to show some problems can\'t be solved by any computer. It\'s the oldest trick in complexity theory, and you can watch it work in a table of 0s and 1s.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/diagonalization-techniques"},{"id":"complexity-zoo/exp-exponential-time","title":"EXP: Exponential Time","description":"Finally, a separation we can actually prove. The Time Hierarchy Theorem says that more time really does let you solve more problems, and it pins down a class of problems that are guaranteed not to be in P.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/complexity-zoo/exp-exponential-time"},{"id":"failed-proofs/the-relativization-barrier","title":"The Relativization Barrier","description":"In 1975, Baker, Gill and Solovay built two imaginary worlds: one where P equals NP, and one where it doesn\'t. Any proof that works the same in both worlds can\'t settle the question. That rules out the most natural attack of all.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/the-relativization-barrier"},{"id":"failed-proofs/why-diagonalization-fails","title":"Why Diagonalization Fails for P vs NP","description":"Building a problem outside P is easy: diagonalization does it in one line. The hard part is building one that is also inside NP. Here are the two reasons the oldest trick can\'t finish the job, and the clever things it can still do.","permalink":"/azeon/p-vs-np/failed-proofs/why-diagonalization-fails"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);